= ψ Dispersion Relations =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = The ψ dispersion relation <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math> — derived by linearizing the master equation around a background field — governs how fast ψ disturbances travel, how far a static ψ field reaches, what frequency a resonator must operate above, and how a ψ metamaterial bends and slows ψ waves; it is the single equation from which all [[Psi-Tech]] device frequency and geometry specifications follow.
| everyday_analogy   = Light in glass travels slower than light in vacuum, bends at surfaces, and cannot propagate below a certain frequency in a metal waveguide. All of these phenomena follow from one dispersion relation connecting frequency to wavelength. The ψ dispersion relation does the same thing for psionic waves — it is the master engineering specification sheet for every [[Psi-Tech]] device before a single component is specified.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A minimum operating frequency <math>\omega_c = m_{\rm eff}</math> below which ψ waves cannot propagate and instead decay exponentially; (2) A coherence length <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff}</math> setting the spatial reach of any static ψ source; (3) A group velocity always less than <math>c</math>, approaching <math>c</math> only for high-frequency ψ waves; (4) A nonlinear stiffening effect in which a trained operator's sustained background field <math>\psi_0</math> increases the local effective mass, shortening the coherence length; (5) A frequency-dependent refractive index enabling ψ metamaterial lens and waveguide design.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The coherence length <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff}</math> is the single most important engineering parameter derived from this page. Every [[Psi-Tech]] device is designed around a target <math>L_\psi</math>, and every [[ψ Metamaterial]] is an engineered structure that sets <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> — and therefore <math>L_\psi</math> — to a design-specified value.
