The ψ dispersion relation is the engineering backbone of [[Psionic Engineering]]. It answers the questions every device designer must ask before specifying geometry, materials, or drive electronics:

* At what frequency must the device operate?
* How far does the ψ field extend from the device?
* At what speed does ψ information travel between components?
* How does the local field environment — set by the operator's own ψ background — shift these answers?

All five questions have exact mathematical answers derived from a single linearization procedure applied to the master psionic field equation. This page carries out that derivation explicitly and translates every result into engineering quantities with device-specific targets.

== Linearization of the Master Equation ==

=== Setup ===

The master psionic field equation in the absence of EM coupling and external sources (vacuum propagation):

:<math>\Box\psi - m^2\psi - \lambda\psi^3 = 0</math>

To find how small perturbations propagate, decompose the field into a spatially uniform, time-constant background <math>\psi_0</math> plus a small perturbation <math>\delta\psi</math>:

:<math>\psi(\mathbf{x},t) = \psi_0 + \delta\psi(\mathbf{x},t), \qquad |\delta\psi| \ll |\psi_0|</math>

The background <math>\psi_0</math> is a constant, so <math>\Box\psi_0 = 0</math>. Substituting into the field equation:

:<math>\Box\delta\psi - m^2(\psi_0 + \delta\psi) - \lambda(\psi_0 + \delta\psi)^3 = 0</math>

Expanding the cubic and collecting background and perturbation terms:

:<math>\underbrace{-m^2\psi_0 - \lambda\psi_0^3}_{\text{background equation}} + \underbrace{\Box\delta\psi - m^2\delta\psi - 3\lambda\psi_0^2\,\delta\psi}_{\text{first order in }\delta\psi} + \mathcal{O}(\delta\psi^2) = 0</math>

The background satisfies its own equation <math>m^2\psi_0 + \lambda\psi_0^3 = 0</math> (static vacuum equilibrium). Dropping this and the higher-order terms:

:<math>\boxed{\Box\delta\psi - \bigl(m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2\bigr)\delta\psi = 0}</math>

=== The Effective Mass ===

Define the '''effective mass''':

:<math>m_{\rm eff}^2 \equiv m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ effective mass
| equation       = <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 = m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>m^2</math> — the bare mass of the ψ field. Sets the base coherence length in vacuum with no background.
* <math>3\lambda\psi_0^2</math> — the nonlinear mass shift. When a sustained background field <math>\psi_0 \neq 0</math> exists — such as the ψ field maintained by a trained operator — the effective mass increases. This stiffens the medium and shortens the coherence length.
* <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> is the quantity that enters every engineering formula on this page. The bare mass <math>m</math> alone is never sufficient for device design.
* '''Key implication:''' A trained operator with sustained <math>\psi_0</math> operates in a stiffer local ψ medium than an untrained person. Devices must be calibrated to the operator's effective mass, not the vacuum bare mass.
}}

The linearized equation becomes the massive Klein–Gordon equation:

:<math>\bigl(\Box - m_{\rm eff}^2\bigr)\delta\psi = 0</math>

This is one of the most well-studied equations in theoretical physics — it governs the Higgs field excitations, the inflaton, and the pion in nuclear physics. Every result derived for those fields applies directly to psionic perturbations.

== The Dispersion Relation ==

Insert the plane-wave ansatz <math>\delta\psi = A\,e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x} - \omega t)}</math> into the linearized equation. With <math>\Box = \partial_t^2 - \nabla^2</math>:

:<math>(-\omega^2 + k^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2)\,\delta\psi = 0</math>

For non-trivial solutions (<math>A \neq 0</math>):

:<math>\boxed{\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ dispersion relation
| equation       = <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>\omega</math> — angular frequency of the ψ wave (radians per second in SI). This is what a [[ψ Resonator]] must be tuned to support.
* <math>k = |\mathbf{k}|</math> — wave number (radians per metre in SI). Related to wavelength by <math>k = 2\pi/\lambda_\psi</math>.
* <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> — effective mass. Acts as a frequency offset: even at <math>k=0</math>, the field oscillates at <math>\omega = m_{\rm eff}</math>.
* '''Read as a sentence:''' "The square of the ψ wave frequency equals the square of its spatial oscillation rate plus a fixed offset set by the local field environment."
* '''Massless limit''' <math>m_{\rm eff} \to 0</math>: <math>\omega = k</math> — ψ propagates exactly at <math>c</math>, identical to a photon or graviton.
* '''Long-wavelength limit''' <math>k \to 0</math>: <math>\omega \to m_{\rm eff}</math> — minimum possible frequency for a propagating ψ wave.
}}

=== The ω–k Diagram ===

The dispersion curve is a hyperbola in the <math>(\omega, k)</math> plane:

{| class="wikitable" style="width:80%; margin:auto;"
! Region !! Condition !! Behaviour !! Engineering Name
|-
| Above curve || <math>\omega > \sqrt{k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}</math> || Evanescent decay, <math>k</math> imaginary || Forbidden band
|-
| On curve || <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math> || Propagating wave || Dispersion relation
|-
| Below cutoff || <math>\omega < m_{\rm eff}</math> || Pure exponential decay, no oscillation || Evanescent / Yukawa regime
|-
| Asymptote (<math>k \gg m_{\rm eff}</math>) || <math>\omega \approx k</math> || Effectively massless, speed → <math>c</math> || Ultra-relativistic ψ limit
|}

The asymptote line <math>\omega = k</math> (the massless case) is always approached from below — massive ψ waves always travel slower than <math>c</math> in all physically meaningful measures. See group velocity below.

== Five Engineering Quantities ==

Every [[Psi-Tech]] device specification reduces to one or more of the following five quantities. All are derived exactly from <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math>.

=== 1. Phase Velocity ===

:<math>v_\phi = \frac{\omega}{k} = \frac{\sqrt{k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}}{k} = \sqrt{1 + \frac{m_{\rm eff}^2}{k^2}} \geq 1</math>

Phase velocity is always greater than or equal to <math>c</math> for massive ψ. This does not violate causality — phase velocity measures the speed of wavefront crests, not information. No signal travels at <math>v_\phi</math>. In engineering, phase velocity matters for resonator tuning: the wavelength inside a ψ cavity is <math>\lambda_\psi = 2\pi/k = 2\pi v_\phi/\omega</math>, which determines the cavity dimension for a given resonant frequency.

=== 2. Group Velocity ===

:<math>v_g = \frac{d\omega}{dk} = \frac{k}{\omega} = \frac{k}{\sqrt{k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}} \leq 1</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ group velocity
| equation       = <math>v_g = \frac{k}{\sqrt{k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2}}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>v_g</math> — the speed at which ψ energy and information travel. Always less than or equal to <math>c</math>.
* Approaches <math>c</math> when <math>k \gg m_{\rm eff}</math>: high-frequency, short-wavelength ψ waves are nearly as fast as light.
* Approaches zero as <math>k \to 0</math>: near-cutoff ψ waves travel very slowly — the group velocity vanishes at the cutoff frequency.
* '''Engineering implication:''' A [[ψ Waveguide]] operating close to its cutoff frequency has very slow group velocity — useful for delay lines and slow-wave structures, but limits bandwidth.
}}

=== 3. Cutoff Frequency ===

:<math>\omega_c = m_{\rm eff} \qquad \Longleftrightarrow \qquad f_c = \frac{m_{\rm eff}}{2\pi} \quad \text{(SI: Hz)}</math>

Below <math>\omega_c</math>, the wave number <math>k</math> becomes imaginary and the plane-wave solution becomes an evanescent wave:

:<math>k = i\kappa, \quad \kappa = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 - \omega^2} \qquad (\omega < \omega_c)</math>

:<math>\delta\psi \propto e^{-\kappa x} \quad \text{(exponential decay, not oscillation)}</math>

No propagating ψ mode exists below the cutoff. Every [[ψ Resonator]] and [[ψ Waveguide]] must operate above <math>\omega_c</math>. Every [[ψ Source]] must emit at <math>\omega > \omega_c</math> to produce a propagating field rather than a locally-decaying one.

=== 4. Coherence Length ===

In the static limit (<math>\omega = 0</math>, time-independent ψ field):

:<math>k^2 = -m_{\rm eff}^2 \implies k = i\,m_{\rm eff}</math>

:<math>\psi_{\rm static}(r) = -\frac{G_\psi M_\psi}{r}\,e^{-m_{\rm eff}\,r}</math>

The field decays to <math>1/e</math> of its source value at the '''coherence length''':

:<math>\boxed{L_\psi = \frac{1}{m_{\rm eff}}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ coherence length
| equation       = <math>L_\psi = \frac{1}{m_{\rm eff}}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>L_\psi</math> — the distance over which a static ψ field remains significant. Beyond <math>L_\psi</math>, it decays exponentially.
* Larger <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> → shorter <math>L_\psi</math> → more localised ψ field. This is the [[Psi Defender]] design principle: high <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> confines the shield tightly around the operator.
* Smaller <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> → longer <math>L_\psi</math> → field extends further. The [[Psi Stabilizer]] targets <math>L_\psi \approx 20</math> cm (spanning the operator's skull) — a specific engineering requirement on <math>m_{\rm eff}</math>.
* <math>m_{\rm eff} \to 0</math>: <math>L_\psi \to \infty</math> — infinite-range massless ψ, analogous to gravity or electrostatics.
}}

=== 5. Refractive Index ===

The ψ refractive index of a medium with effective mass <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> is:

:<math>n_\psi = \frac{c}{v_\phi} = \frac{k}{\omega} = \sqrt{1 - \frac{\omega_c^2}{\omega^2}} = \sqrt{1 - \frac{m_{\rm eff}^2}{\omega^2}}</math>

For <math>\omega > \omega_c</math>: <math>0 \leq n_\psi < 1</math> — ψ waves in a massive medium have refractive index less than one, analogous to EM waves in a plasma. Snell's law applies at interfaces: <math>n_1 \sin\theta_1 = n_2 \sin\theta_2</math>. A [[ψ Metamaterial]] with engineered <math>m_{\rm eff}(\mathbf{x})</math> is therefore a graded-index ψ lens.

== The Nonlinear Mass Shift: Operator Field Stiffening ==

The effective mass formula <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 = m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math> has a direct physical consequence for device calibration. A trained operator sustaining a background field <math>\psi_0</math> operates inside a locally stiffer medium than an untrained operator with <math>\psi_0 \approx 0</math>.

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = How background field ψ₀ stiffens the local ψ medium
| step1_label = Baseline operator (untrained)
| step1_text  = <math>\psi_0 \approx 0</math>. Effective mass is approximately the bare mass: <math>m_{\rm eff} \approx m</math>. Coherence length <math>L_\psi \approx 1/m</math>. All device design targets apply at this baseline.
| step2_label = Operator in HRV coherence (resting trained)
| step2_text  = Sustained low-amplitude coherent oscillation. <math>\psi_0</math> is small but non-zero. <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 = m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math> slightly exceeds <math>m^2</math>. Coherence length decreases slightly. Device must compensate via ferrofluid <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> adjustment (see [[ψ Permeability]]).
| step3_label = Operator in deep Anchor mode (active session)
| step3_text  = Sustained high-amplitude ψ background. <math>\psi_0</math> significant. If <math>3\lambda\psi_0^2 \gg m^2</math>, effective mass is dominated by the nonlinear term: <math>m_{\rm eff} \approx \sqrt{3\lambda}\,\psi_0</math>. Coherence length contracts proportionally. The [[Anchor Controller]] must track this shift via HRV-derived coherence state.
| step4_label = Group ritual or PsiNet node cluster
