== Connection to OSGS Symbolic Geometry ==

The [[OSGS|Octahedral Symbolic Geometry System]] defines six primitive symbols (Point, Line, Circle, Wave, Angle, Curve) that may correspond to fundamental ψ mode topologies — see [[ψ Metamaterials]] (B9) for the full argument. The dispersion relation provides the frequency axis for this correspondence:

* '''Wave''' primitive: corresponds to a propagating ψ mode on the dispersion curve, <math>\omega > \omega_c</math>, <math>k</math> real.
* '''Point''' primitive: corresponds to the static Yukawa solution, <math>\omega = 0</math>, <math>k = im_{\rm eff}</math> — a localised source.
* '''Circle''' primitive: corresponds to a closed resonant mode, quantised <math>k</math> from [[ψ Boundary Conditions]].

This connection is speculative (Track B) but provides a mathematical framework linking the symbolic geometry system to engineered dispersion properties. Each primitive symbol may function as a boundary-condition operator that selects a specific region of the <math>(\omega, k)</math> dispersion diagram.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Dispersion in the Presence of Gravity ===

In curved spacetime (strong gravitational fields, relevant to [[Magnetogravitic Planetary Anchoring]]):

:<math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2 + \xi R</math>

where <math>\xi</math> is a non-minimal coupling to the Ricci scalar <math>R</math> and the term <math>\xi R</math> acts as an additional mass shift. Near a planetary surface, <math>R \approx -8\pi G T^\mu_\mu</math> from Einstein's equations. This gravitational dispersion correction is negligible for HelmKit-scale devices but relevant to vehicle-scale ψ systems.

=== Tachyonic Instability ===

If <math>m_{\rm eff}^2 < 0</math> (which can occur if <math>m^2 < 0</math> and <math>3\lambda\psi_0^2</math> does not compensate), the dispersion relation gives imaginary <math>\omega</math> for small <math>k</math> — exponential growth rather than oscillation. This is a tachyonic instability, analogous to the Higgs mechanism before symmetry breaking. In the psionic context, an unstable ψ background would indicate that <math>\psi_0 = 0</math> is not the true vacuum — the field would roll to a new stable value. The [[Anchor Controller]] monitors for tachyonic precursor signatures (anomalous HRV variance growth) as a safety interlock.

